judgment.answer:
A corrected version is:

```html
<p>Write a program to find the largest number among 3 numbers.</p>
<input type="text" placeholder="Enter 1st number" id="t1">
<br>
<input type="text" placeholder="Enter 2nd number" id="t2">
<br>
<input type="text" placeholder="Enter 3rd number" id="t3">
<button onclick="check()">Check</button>
<h3 id="ans">The largest number is</h3>

<script>
function check() {
    const n1 = Number(document.getElementById("t1").value);
    const n2 = Number(document.getElementById("t2").value);
    const n3 = Number(document.getElementById("t3").value);

    const largest = Math.max(n1, n2, n3);
    document.getElementById("ans").textContent = "The largest is " + largest;
}
</script>
```

Assistant A correctly identifies the main errors: `getAnimations` should be `getElementById`, and the output refers to `num1`, `num2`, and `num3` instead of the declared variables. It also correctly converts the input strings to numbers. Its comparison logic works for ties as well as distinct values, so the answer is helpful and relevant.

Assistant B does not fix the code. It incorrectly interprets “among 3 numbers” as asking for a number greater than 3, and its response is in Portuguese despite the user asking in English. A is therefore substantially more accurate and useful. [[A>>B]]

